def note_page(n):
 if n['slug']=='traffic-aware-placement':
  body='''<p>Microservice applications spend part of their time communicating. When two services run on different hosts, their interaction crosses a network whose behavior can change. A placement decision therefore has to connect two views: the application’s communication pattern and the infrastructure carrying it.</p><h2>Traffic describes demand on a path</h2><p>A call graph tells us which services interact. Adding traffic volume tells us which interactions are likely to matter most. A pair of services that exchanges large responses can have a different communication profile from a pair that sends small requests frequently.</p><p>However, traffic alone does not tell us what an interaction costs on a particular placement. The same two services can experience different delays depending on the hosts assigned to them.</p><h2>The network gives traffic a location-dependent cost</h2><p>A useful simplified objective weights inter-service traffic by the delay between the hosts carrying that traffic. This is an optimization proxy: it helps compare candidate placements, while the application’s measured response time remains an outcome to evaluate.</p><div class="article-flow"><div><strong>Application view</strong><span>Who communicates, in which direction, and with how much traffic?</span></div><div><strong>Infrastructure view</strong><span>Which hosts are involved, and what network conditions connect them?</span></div><div><strong>Placement decision</strong><span>Which feasible assignment reduces communication cost?</span></div></div><h2>Direction matters</h2><p>A small request can trigger a large response. Retry behavior, acknowledgments, and back-pressure can also make the two directions behave differently. Modeling each direction explicitly avoids assuming that every interaction is symmetric.</p><p>TraDE incorporates forward and reverse traffic into its communication objective. This gives the scheduler a way to reason about asymmetry instead of collapsing the interaction into a single unqualified edge.</p><h2>Adaptation still needs a reason to move</h2><p>Even a better placement has an execution cost. A scheduler needs to consider resource feasibility, measurement quality, and the disruption associated with moving services. The question is whether a placement change produces a useful application-level benefit under the conditions being observed.</p><div class="article-callout">The research question connects traffic, network conditions, feasible placement, and measured application behavior.</div><p>This note introduces the intuition behind <a href="/projects/trade/">TraDE</a>. The <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TPDS.2025.3626424" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">paper</a> provides the model, scheduling method, and experimental evaluation.</p>'''
 else:
  body='''<p>Two scheduling policies can appear to disagree simply because they experienced different workloads or network conditions. A repeatable experiment makes those conditions explicit so that the comparison is easier to interpret.</p><h2>Define the scenario before comparing policies</h2><p>Start with the application, workload, infrastructure, and behavior you want to study. Record what changes, when it changes, and which conditions remain fixed across policy runs.</p><ul><li>Application version, deployment configuration, and resource requests.</li><li>Request rates, request mix, replay duration, and workload generation settings.</li><li>Network delay, bandwidth settings, topology, and the timing of changes.</li><li>Policy settings, initialization, measurement windows, and run order.</li></ul><h2>Check that the intended conditions actually happened</h2><p>A configured delay is an input to the emulation mechanism. The delay experienced by an application is a measurement. Validating the relationship between them is part of evaluating the experimental environment.</p><p>The same applies to bandwidth limits and workload generation. If an intended input is not achieved, the experiment may still produce a result, but it answers a different question from the one originally posed.</p><h2>Measure the application and the mechanism</h2><div class="article-flow"><div><strong>Inputs</strong><span>Workloads, network conditions, deployment state, and policy settings.</span></div><div><strong>Mechanism</strong><span>Telemetry, emulation accuracy, decision behavior, and scheduling overhead.</span></div><div><strong>Outcomes</strong><span>Response time, throughput, successful requests, and resource behavior.</span></div></div><p>Application response time describes what users experience. Scheduler overhead and migration behavior help explain how the policy produced that result. Emulation accuracy helps establish what environment the policy was actually tested in.</p><h2>Preserve enough context to repeat the comparison</h2><p>Keep configuration, code versions, raw observations, analysis scripts, and the interpretation of each metric together. Repeating a run is most useful when the next reader can understand both the experiment and the conditions attached to its conclusions.</p><div class="article-callout">Reproducibility is a property of the complete experimental workflow: the scenario, the mechanism, the measurements, and their recorded context.</div><p>These concerns motivate <a href="/projects/idynamics/">iDynamics</a>, a framework for evaluating microservice scheduling under controllable cloud–edge dynamics. See the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TSC.2026.3735871" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">paper</a> and <a href="https://github.com/Cloudslab/iDynamics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">implementation</a> for the research framework.</p>'''
 return layout(n['title'],n['summary'],f'''<main id="main"><section class="page-hero"><div class="container article-shell"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/notes/">Research notes</a><span>/</span><span>Explanatory note</span></div><div class="article-header"><div class="note-meta">{n['tag']} · {n['reading']}</div><h1>{n['title']}</h1><p class="lead">{n['summary']}</p></div><article class="prose">{body}<p class="source-note">An explanatory summary of the linked research. Published on this website in November 2026.</p><div class="related-work"><a href="/notes/">All notes</a><a href="/#work">Explore the research</a></div></article></div></section></main>''','notes')

